    tldr: "Primary cutaneous marginal zone lymphoma is a very slow-growing lymphoma that appears as pink or purple lumps in the skin and rarely spreads inside the body (4 in 100 people in one series). The WHO classification made it an entity of its own in 2022; surgery or radiotherapy clears most lesions, and relapses in the skin are common, in about 44 in 100 people.",
    summary: "WHO-HAEM5 designated primary cutaneous marginal zone lymphoma a separate entity, where it was previously included under extranodal marginal zone lymphoma of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, because of its distinctive clinicopathological features, and kept the word lymphoma (Alaggio 2022). The name primary cutaneous marginal zone lymphoproliferative disorder, also in use, is not the WHO-HAEM5 name, and its source could not be re-read on 18 November 2026. In 137 patients, 51 percent had a solitary lesion, 29 percent regional and 20 percent generalised skin disease; surgical excision, local radiotherapy or both were the initial treatment in 86 percent, complete remission followed in 88 percent (93 percent of solitary or localised and 71 percent of multifocal cases), cutaneous relapses occurred in 53 patients (44 percent), median disease-free survival was 47 months, overall survival was 93 percent at five and at ten years, and six patients (4 percent) developed extracutaneous disease (J Am Acad Dermatol 2013). Unlike other marginal zone lymphomas, 19 of 49 primary cutaneous cases with plasmacytic differentiation (39 percent) expressed IgG4, which the authors called the highest rate reported to that date in any B-cell lymphoma, against 1 of 120 non-cutaneous cases (Modern Pathology 2013).\n\nHow it differs from its parent: it is confined to the skin at diagnosis, is treated by dermatologists with excision or radiotherapy rather than systemic therapy, and its relapses are skin-only; extracutaneous spread is the exception. Borrelia infection has been implicated in some European cases.\n\nHow common: no registry share in the sources read.\n\nTreatment: excision or low-dose radiotherapy for solitary or localised lesions; observation, intralesional steroids or rituximab for multifocal disease; systemic therapy only for the rare extracutaneous spread, following the parent page (J Am Acad Dermatol 2013; Zucca 2020).",
